            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"Thanks\" — and it literally offers someone grace."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "grazie = GRAH-tsyeh: z in Italian is ts; the final -e is sounded (so -zie is two beats)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "grazie (\"thanks\") from Latin gratiae, plural of gratia (\"favour, thanks, grace\"). It's the same gratia behind English grace, gratitude, grateful, gratis (\"free\"), congratulate, and ingratiate. So grazie literally wishes someone grace."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The reply is prego (\"you're welcome\") — literally \"I pray [it's nothing],\" from Latin precari (\"to pray,\" becomes English pray, precarious). You give thanks/grace; they wave it off with a prayer."
            }
          ]
        },
